{"engine_version":"0.29.0","file":"leo-resilience-gnss-jammed-leo-carries.toml","toml":"# LEO-PNT resilience mission: GNSS jammed, LEO-PNT carries the user, integrity maintained.\n#\n# Three phases on one 30 s timeline, each running a `spectrum` receiver (what it can still\n# track) and an `integrity` run (receiver autonomous integrity monitoring, RAIM, against a\n# 40 m horizontal and 50 m vertical alert limit):\n#\n#   nominal   GPS L1 C/A, Galileo E5a and a generic low Earth orbit (LEO) positioning,\n#             navigation and timing (PNT) layer in the S and C bands all track; integrity\n#             uses the GNSS layer (24 satellites at 20 200 km) and the LEO layer (240 at\n#             1080 km, 53 deg, and 72 at 1080 km, 97 deg) together.\n#   jammed    two barrage jammers, one over L1 and one over L5/E5, at -95 dBW received:\n#             both GNSS signals fall below the 25 dB-Hz tracking floor; the S- and C-band\n#             LEO signals are untouched. Integrity runs on the LEO layer alone.\n#   recovery  the jammers stop; both layers again.\n#\n# The alarm channel is the integrity kind's own: raised at epochs where the protection\n# level cannot be formed or exceeds the alert limit. With GNSS jammed the LEO layer alone\n# still forms a protection level at every epoch; it is under the 50 m alert limit at 28\n# of the 30 grid times and the alarm is raised at the other two. That is integrity\n# working: the user is warned whenever the bound is too large, and availability, not\n# integrity, is what the jamming costs.\n#\n# The integrity runs use that kind's own user track (a circular track 0.4 km up, the\n# convention of the other bundled integrity runs), not the spectrum receiver's site, and\n# a 1 m user-equivalent range error for both layers; those are scenario choices, stated\n# here. The integrity kind does not see the jammer: the jammed phase's LEO-only\n# constellation is the campaign's statement of what the spectrum run says is left.\n# MODELLED.\n